-- scripts/dst/corpus_rotating_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A15 commit 2 acceptance, under ADR-001 D11: THE SCHEDULED ROTATING CORPUS.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- TWO ENTRY POINTS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS THE JOB
-- ============================================================================
--
--   `main`           the acceptance suite. Runs in `make dst`.
--   `scheduled_run`  THE JOB CI ACTUALLY RUNS. It resolves the epoch, derives
--                    the window, runs its seeds through the real driver,
--                    validates the corpus and exits non-zero on any rejection.
--
-- They are separate because the thing that has to fail on a zero, truncated or
-- below-minimum window is the JOB, not a test of the job. `make corpus_rotating`
-- forces each of those three conditions through `scheduled_run` and requires a
-- non-zero exit — demonstrated rather than asserted, which is the acceptance
-- clause A16 established pays for itself (four of eight scripts turned out to
-- have no failing exit path at all).
--
-- ============================================================================
-- D11 ASKS FOR TWO PROPERTIES AND A FROZEN WINDOW SATISFIES ONE PERFECTLY
-- ============================================================================
--
-- "a scheduled rotating corpus whose seed window CHANGES DETERMINISTICALLY and
-- is REPORTED."
--
-- The failure this suite exists against is not a window that is wrong; it is a
-- window that never moves. Such a window is:
--
--   perfectly deterministic       the same seeds every scheduled run
--   byte-identical when reported  which reads as stability
--   green under every gate        because nothing else in the tree asserts
--                                 that a window MOVES
--
-- and it searches one neighbourhood forever. Determinism is 0-for-27 across
-- thirteen clusters and it is the property a frozen thing has in the highest
-- degree.
--
-- So the rotation is checked three ways, and the three are not redundant:
--
--   POSITION BY POSITION   `rotation-position-frozen`. A whole-window
--                          comparison is green on "a fixed prefix plus a
--                          rotating tail" — that window changes at every epoch
--                          and is pinned at every prefix position. Only a
--                          per-position check sees it.
--   THE WRAP BRANCH        `rotation-wrap-not-exercised`. S8's complement: the
--                          rotation wraps at the end of the space, and a sample
--                          that never crosses the boundary leaves that branch
--                          unwalked. An unwalked branch is not pinned, it is
--                          ABSENT, and absent reads identically to unchanged.
--   THE INPUT'S ROUTE      `epoch-not-resolved`. The sharpest of the three,
--                          because it defeats the other two without touching
--                          them: if the scheduler's epoch is absent and the job
--                          DEFAULTS it, every rotation check stays green — the
--                          rotation is fine, the input stopped arriving — and
--                          the window is frozen. `resolve_epoch` has no
--                          default, and `make corpus_rotating` runs the job
--                          with the variable UNSET and requires it to fail.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHY THE SEEDS ARE ACTUALLY RUN AND NOT ONLY DERIVED
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Everything above is pure arithmetic and would pass over a window whose seeds
-- no driver can execute. So the job runs its window through the real driver,
-- and `make dst` runs it at the DEMO scale (4 seeds off a 26-seed space) while
-- the scheduled CI job runs it at the full one (240 off 1024). Same
-- `rotating_window`, same `validate_rotation`, same shard partition, different
-- declared minimum — and the workflow is grepped to prove the scheduled job
-- does not quietly select the demo scale, which would report a rotating corpus
-- while searching four seeds.

